      "side": 1,
      "message": "I took a look at the generated code (for pixel) and found that unnamed structs appear to do what we want. With this decl:\n\n  struct binder_node {\n\tint debug_id;\n\tspinlock_t lock;\n\tstruct binder_work work;\n\tunion {\n\t\tstruct rb_node rb_node;\n\t\tstruct hlist_node dead_node;\n\t};\n\tstruct binder_proc *proc;\n\tstruct hlist_head refs;\n\tint internal_strong_refs;\n\tint local_weak_refs;\n\tint local_strong_refs;\n\tint tmp_refs;\n\tbinder_uintptr_t ptr;\n\tbinder_uintptr_t cookie;\n\tstruct {\n\t\tunsigned has_strong_ref:1;\n\t\tunsigned pending_strong_ref:1;\n\t\tunsigned has_weak_ref:1;\n\t\tunsigned pending_weak_ref:1;\n\t};\n\tstruct {\n\t\tunsigned has_async_transaction:1;\n\t\tunsigned accept_fds:1;\n\t\tunsigned min_priority:8;\n\t};\n\tstruct binder_worklist async_todo;\n  };\n\nThe compiler code placed the second unnamed struct at an offset of 4 bytes from the first one and used 1-byte ld/st to manipulate the single-bit bitfields.\n\nLooking at structure size... the struct binder_node was 144 bytes for all three declarations that I tried:\n\n1) original declaration with no unnamed structs and the bitfields grouped together\n2) declaration shown above with unnamed structs and elements declared as \"unsigned\"\n3) same as #2, but elements declared as uint8_t",
